@article{ WOS:001454915700001,
Author = {Cockle, Kristina L. and Cuatianquiz Lima, Cecilia and Nunez Montellano,
   Maria Gabriela and Bonaparte, Eugenia Bianca and Zaffignani, Daniela and
   Juncosa Polzella, Agostina S. and Radi, Blas and Ferreira-Xavier,
   Hipolito D. and da Hora, Jessica S. and Di Sallo, Facundo G. and
   Soto-Patino, Juliana and Lopez, Fernando G. and Zulian, Viviane and
   Mino, I, Carolina and Perez, Moira and Norris, Andrea R. and Schaufler,
   Maria Laura and Grp OrnitologiaS, grupo},
Title = {A framework for sharing power in research teams and promoting justice in
   scientific publication},
Journal = {ORNITHOLOGICAL APPLICATIONS},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {127},
Number = {2},
Month = {MAY 1},
Abstract = {Many ornithologists seek tools to work more equitably with people from
   historically marginalized and exploited groups. We developed a process
   to promote the collective construction of ornithological knowledge in
   the Special Feature series ``Ecology and conservation of cavity nesters
   in the Neotropics'' for the journals Ornithology and Ornithological
   Applications. Colonialism produces systems that consecrate Eurocentric
   ideas from dominant nations (including Canada, the USA, and countries of
   Western Europe) and reinforce hierarchies of power between and within
   regions. Recognizing these systems, we proposed a Special Feature to
   support, highlight, and connect teams from Latin America, a region
   historically exploited by imperial powers. We adopted sociocracy, a
   governance model that promotes the sharing of power, to create the
   proposal and organize the call for papers and to write a Review article
   and this Perspective article. We adopted and developed transparent,
   consent-based decision-making processes, including a process for
   determining authorship order. We crafted open invitations, used
   collective proposals (structured brainstorming), encouraged citation of
   work from regional journals, tested a system for manuscript submission
   and review in Spanish, and introduced mechanisms for multi-way feedback.
   This framework helped reduce some barriers commonly faced by
   historically marginalized authors, distribute power more equitably, and
   recognize a broader diversity of contributions to ornithology. Despite
   these efforts, several challenges remained. For example, the publishing
   interests of Ornithology, Ornithological Applications, and most
   high-impact ornithological journals are often poorly aligned with
   current research priorities in many parts of Latin America (e.g.,
   reproductive biology of endangered species). We encourage scientists at
   all career stages, technicians, and non-academics to reflect on their
   citation politics (which sources they cite, and how their citation
   practice may unintentionally reproduce inequities), and to implement
   collective workflows that promote equitable sharing of power within
   research teams. Translated versions of this article are available in
   Supplementary Material 1 (Spanish) and Supplementary Material 2
   (Portuguese).
   center dot Colonialism unfairly and detrimentally excludes people and
   ideas from Western science.center dot Many ornithologists seek tools to
   include historically excluded and exploited groups.center dot To
   organize the Special Feature series ``Ecology and conservation of
   cavity-nesters in the Neotropics{''} we adopted sociocracy, a governance
   model that intentionally shares power.center dot We tested and reflected
   on various actions to reduce barriers imposed by colonialism and thus
   increase the inclusion of people and worldviews from the
   Neotropics.center dot To write a review article, eight of us used
   collective proposals, consent-based decisions, critical citation
   politics, writing and reviewing templates, discussions, and feedback in
   rounds.center dot These tools redistributed power and promoted the
   active, equitable, and consensual participation of researchers from
   marginalized groups at various career stages.center dot Motivated by the
   need for political and epistemic justice, we encourage colleagues to
   adopt collective, equitable work systems that lead toward just,
   hospitable, and pluralistic ornithologies.
   Muchas personas dedicadas a la ornitolog \& iacute;a buscan herramientas
   para trabajar de forma m \& aacute;s equitativa con personas de grupos
   hist \& oacute;ricamente marginados y explotados. Desarrollamos un
   proceso para promover la construcci \& oacute;n colectiva de
   conocimiento ornitol \& oacute;gico en la colecci \& oacute;n especial
   ``Ecology and conservation of cavity nesters in the Neotropics'' (Ecolog
   \& iacute;a y conservaci \& oacute;n de aves que anidan en cavidades en
   el Neotr \& oacute;pico) para las revistas Ornithology y Ornithological
   Applications. El colonialismo produce sistemas que consagran las ideas
   euroc \& eacute;ntricas provenientes de naciones dominantes (incluidas
   Canad \& aacute;, EE.UU. y los pa \& iacute;ses de Europa occidental) y
   refuerzan jerarqu \& iacute;as de poder tanto entre las regiones como
   dentro de ellas. Reconociendo estos sistemas, propusimos una colecci \&
   oacute;n especial para apoyar, destacar y conectar a equipos de Am \&
   eacute;rica Latina, una regi \& oacute;n hist \& oacute;ricamente
   explotada por las potencias imperiales. Adoptamos la sociocracia, un
   modelo de gobernanza que promueve el reparto de poder, para crear la
   propuesta, organizar la convocatoria de art \& iacute;culos, escribir un
   art \& iacute;culo de revisi \& oacute;n y escribir este art \&
   iacute;culo de perspectiva. Adoptamos y desarrollamos procesos de toma
   de decisiones transparentes y basados en el consentimiento, incluyendo
   un proceso para determinar el orden de autor \& iacute;a. Elaboramos
   invitaciones abiertas, utilizamos propuestas colectivas (tormenta de
   ideas estructurada), fomentamos la citaci \& oacute;n de trabajos de
   revistas regionales, probamos un sistema de env \& iacute;o y revisi \&
   oacute;n de manuscritos en castellano e introdujimos mecanismos para la
   retroalimentaci \& oacute;n multidireccional. Este marco ayud \& oacute;
   a reducir algunas barreras enfrentadas por personas autoras hist \&
   oacute;ricamente marginadas, a distribuir el poder de forma m \&
   aacute;s equitativa y a reconocer una mayor diversidad de contribuciones
   a la ornitolog \& iacute;a. A pesar de estos esfuerzos, subsisten varios
   desaf \& iacute;os. Por ejemplo, los intereses editoriales de
   Ornithology, Ornithological Applications y la mayor \& iacute;a de las
   revistas ornitol \& oacute;gicas de alto impacto a menudo est \&
   aacute;n poco alineados con las prioridades actuales de investigaci \&
   oacute;n en muchas partes de Am \& eacute;rica Latina (por ejemplo,
   biolog \& iacute;a reproductiva de especies en peligro de extinci \&
   oacute;n). Alentamos a las personas cient \& iacute;ficas en todas las
   etapas de su carrera, personas t \& eacute;cnicas y personas no acad \&
   eacute;micas a reflexionar sobre sus pol \& iacute;ticas de citaci \&
   oacute;n (cu \& aacute;les fuentes citan y c \& oacute;mo su pr \&
   aacute;ctica de citaci \& oacute;n puede reproducir inequidades de
   manera no intencional), y a implementar flujos de trabajo colectivos que
   promuevan un reparto equitativo del poder dentro de los equipos de
   investigaci \& oacute;n. Versiones traducidas de este art \& iacute;culo
   se encuentran en (castellano) y (portugu \& eacute;s).},
