Month = {FEB},
Abstract = {Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (IPF) is an epithelial-driven interstitial
   lung disease of unknown etiology characterized by the excessive
   proliferation of fibroblast populations that synthesize large amounts of
   extracellular matrix. In this devastating disorder, all aging hallmarks
   appear prematurely or are altered. This review highlights key findings
   about IPF characteristics recently recognized as hallmarks of aging,
   including mechanical alterations, inflammaging, dysbiosis, alternative
   splicing, and disabled macroautophagy. It also revisits the classic
   hallmarks of aging, which encompass stem cell exhaustion, cellular
   senescence, and altered intercellular communication. Enhancing our
   understanding of the fundamental processes that underlie the altered
   hallmarks of aging in IPF may facilitate the development of innovative
   experimental strategies to improve therapeutic outcomes.},
